##DESCRIPTION
## Statistics: Confidence intervals
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by sawblade

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Confidence intervals)
## DBsection(One sample proportion)
## Institution(ASU)
## MLT(CI_1p_04)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(2)
## KEYWORDS('large sample','confidence interval','statistics', 'population proportion')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',         'PGML.pl',
    'parserRadioButtons.pl', 'PGstatisticsmacros.pl',
    'contextPercent.pl',     'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Numeric');

$a  = random(300, 400);
$b  = random(500, 600, 25);
$p0 = $a / $b;

$conf  = list_random(90, 95, 98, 99);
$alpha = (100 - $conf) / 100;
$z     = normal_distr(0.5 - $alpha / 2);

$ME = sqrt($p0 * (1 - $p0) / $b);
($p1, $p2) = ($p0 - $ME, $p0 + $ME);

Context('Percent');
$p2pct = Percent($p2);

$rb = RadioButtons([ 'larger', 'smaller', 'same' ], 1, labels => 'ABC');

BEGIN_PGML
A poll is taken in which [$a] out of [$b] randomly selected voters indicated
their preference for a certain candidate. Round answers to three digits 
(e.g. 0.352 or 35.2%). 

a) Find the margin of error for this [$conf]% confidence interval for [`p`]:

    [`ME=`] [_]{$ME}

b) Find a [$conf]% confidence interval for [`p`].  

    [_]{Percent($p1)} [`\leq p \leq`] [_]{$p2pct}

c) Without doing any calculations, indicate whether the margin of error is larger or smaller or the same for an 80% confidence interval. [_]{$rb}

END_PGML

$p00 = Round($p0, 4);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First, note that the sample proportion is [`` \hat{p} = \frac{[$a]}{[$b]} = [$p00]. ``]

a) The margin of error for a proportion is given by 
[``` ME = \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n}} = \sqrt{\frac{([$p00])(1-[$p00])}{[$b]}} = [@ Round($ME,4) @] ```]

b) The [$conf]% confidence interval is given by [`(\hat{p}-ME, \hat{p}+ME)`] 
or [` ([@Round($p1,4)@],[@ Round($p2,4)@])`]

c) If a smaller confidence level is sought, the the margin of error is *smaller*. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.